\title{Signed Lattice-State Models for Alexander Polynomials:\\Positivity, Universality, Products, and Reciprocity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We determine the precise scope of interpreting Alexander polynomials as area-generating functions of finite lattice-state families. A monotone square-lattice path from $(0,0)$ to $(n,n)$ is encoded by a balanced word with $n$ east and $n$ north steps, and its area is the number of east-before-north pairs. Any forbidden-region model selects a finite subset of these paths, so its unsigned area generating function has nonnegative coefficients. This refutes the unsigned interpretation for an infinite knot family: for every $k\geq1$, the normalized Alexander polynomial of $T(2,2k+1)$ has coefficient $-1$ in degree $k-1$, and hence cannot equal any unsigned forbidden-region path generating function, even when the forbidden rule is an arbitrary predicate on paths.

We give the exact correction. Every finitely supported integer coefficient function is representable by a finite signed state sum, whereas unsigned finite state sums represent precisely the finitely supported nonnegative coefficient functions. Product state spaces realize Cauchy products through additive areas and multiplicative signs, while a sign-preserving, area-negating involution implies Laurent reciprocity. For $T(2,2k+1)$ we establish the alternating formula, palindromicity, normalization at $t=1$, the determinant evaluation $|\Delta(-1)|=2k+1$, failure of unsigned representability, and signed representability. Thus cancellation is exactly the algebraic ingredient absent from ordinary path counting.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Alexander polynomial assigns to an oriented knot $K$ an integer Laurent polynomial $\Delta_K(t)$. After symmetric normalization it satisfies the reciprocal relation $\Delta_K(t)=\Delta_K(t^{-1})$, and a standard normalization gives $\Delta_K(1)=1$. Unlike an ordinary counting polynomial, its coefficients may be negative.

A natural combinatorial proposal associates a knot diagram with a region in a square lattice and sums $t^{A(p)}$ over monotone paths avoiding that region. Such a formula would transform topology into enumeration and would resemble a partition function whose grading records area. The reduced trefoil polynomial
\[
 t^{-1}-1+t
\]
already exposes the difficulty: an unsigned census cannot contain minus one object.

We first turn this observation into a theorem that covers arbitrary path-deletion rules and the entire family $T(2,2k+1)$. We then replace ordinary counting by signed counting and characterize both models exactly. Finally, we establish product and symmetry principles that have the same algebraic form as connected-sum multiplicativity and Alexander reciprocity. The distinction between abstract representation and a canonical diagram-local construction remains essential: universality proves expressive completeness, but it does not by itself explain how states arise from crossings.

\section{Coefficient functions and finite state sums}

\begin{definition}
An integer coefficient function is a map $c:\Z\to\Z$. It is \emph{finitely supported} if $c(m)=0$ outside a finite subset of $\Z$. It is \emph{nonnegative} if $c(m)\geq0$ for every $m$, and \emph{palindromic} if $c(m)=c(-m)$ for every $m$.
\end{definition}

A finitely supported coefficient function represents the Laurent polynomial $\sum_m c(m)t^m$.

\begin{definition}
Let $S$ be finite and let $a:S\to\Z$. The \emph{unsigned area generating function} is
\[
 G_{S,a}(t)=\sum_{s\in S}t^{a(s)},
 \qquad
 G_{S,a}(m)=\#\{s\in S:a(s)=m\}.
\]
If $\sigma:S\to\Z$ is a weight map, the \emph{signed state sum} is
\[
 F_{S,\sigma,a}(t)=\sum_{s\in S}\sigma(s)t^{a(s)},
 \qquad
 F_{S,\sigma,a}(m)=\sum_{\substack{s\in S\\a(s)=m}}\sigma(s).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity of unsigned state sums]
Every coefficient of an unsigned finite area generating function is a nonnegative integer.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For fixed $m$, the coefficient is the cardinality of the finite fiber $\{s\in S:a(s)=m\}$. Cardinalities are nonnegative integers.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unsigned sums are signed sums]
Every unsigned area generating function is obtained as a signed state sum by taking $\sigma(s)=1$ for all $s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Summing $1$ over each area fiber gives its cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization of unsigned representability]
A coefficient function $c:\Z\to\Z$ is represented by an unsigned finite area generating function if and only if it is finitely supported and nonnegative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Necessity follows from positivity and from finiteness of the image of the area map. Conversely, choose a finite set $B$ containing the support. For every $m\in B$, create the states $(m,j)$ with $0\leq j<c(m)$ and assign each one area $m$. Exactly $c(m)$ states then occur in degree $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universality of signed state sums]
A coefficient function $c:\Z\to\Z$ is represented by a finite signed state sum if and only if it is finitely supported. All representing weights may be chosen in $\{-1,1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A finite state sum is supported on the finite image of its area map. Conversely, for every $m$ with $c(m)\neq0$, create $|c(m)|$ states of area $m$ and give each weight $\sgn(c(m))$. Their contribution in degree $m$ is
\[
 \sgn(c(m))|c(m)|=c(m).
\]
The union of the finitely many fibers is the desired state family.
\end{proof}

Thus passing from unsigned to signed enumeration removes precisely the nonnegativity restriction. Moreover, among representations using unit signs, the construction is degreewise minimal: a sum of fewer than $|c(m)|$ unit contributions cannot have magnitude $|c(m)|$.

\section{Balanced lattice paths and their area}

\begin{definition}
A monotone square path of size $n$ runs from $(0,0)$ to $(n,n)$ using east steps $E=(1,0)$ and north steps $N=(0,1)$. Equivalently, it is a word $p_1\cdots p_{2n}$ containing $n$ letters $E$ and $n$ letters $N$. Its east-before-north area is
\[
 A(p)=\#\{(i,j):1\leq i<j\leq2n,\ p_i=E,\ p_j=N\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The statistic lies between $0$ and $n^2$. The path $N^nE^n$ has area $0$, while $E^nN^n$ has area $n^2$. Geometrically it counts unit cells on one fixed side of the staircase.

A forbidden rule is any predicate on the finite path set. This is intentionally more permissive than avoidance of a geometric set of cells. Let $L$ be the paths that survive the rule and define
\[
 P_L(t)=\sum_{p\in L}t^{A(p)}.
\]

\begin{corollary}[Positivity under arbitrary path deletion]
For every square size and every forbidden rule, all coefficients of $P_L(t)$ are nonnegative integers.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply positivity of unsigned state sums to $L$ with area map $A$.
\end{proof}

When all paths are retained, the area distribution is the Gaussian binomial coefficient $\binom{2n}{n}_t$ under this convention. Its positivity illustrates both the attraction and the limitation of ordinary path enumeration.

\section{The torus-knot obstruction}

For $k\geq0$, write the symmetric Alexander polynomial of $T(2,2k+1)$ as
\[
 \Delta_k(t)=\sum_{i=-k}^{k}(-1)^{i+k}t^i.
\]
For $k=1$ this is the reduced trefoil polynomial $t^{-1}-1+t$.

\begin{lemma}[A uniform negative coefficient]
For every $k\geq1$, the coefficient of $t^{k-1}$ in $\Delta_k(t)$ is $-1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The exponent $k-1$ lies in $[-k,k]$, and $(k-1)+k=2k-1$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite-family obstruction to unsigned state counting]
For every $k\geq1$, the polynomial $\Delta_k(t)$ is not the unsigned area generating function of any finite state family, for any integer area map.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemma supplies a coefficient $-1$, while every unsigned coefficient is nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite-family obstruction to forbidden-region path counting]
For every $k\geq1$, every $n\geq0$, and every forbidden rule on monotone paths from $(0,0)$ to $(n,n)$, the resulting unsigned area generating function differs from $\Delta_k(t)$. In particular, no such model produces the trefoil polynomial.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Arbitrary path deletion still leaves an unsigned finite state family and hence a nonnegative coefficient function. The coefficient in degree $k-1$ of $\Delta_k$ is negative.
\end{proof}

The result does not depend on a restrictive geometric interpretation of ``forbidden region.'' Even arbitrary selection of balanced paths cannot repair the sign mismatch.

\begin{theorem}[Signed representability of the torus family]
For every $k\geq0$, $\Delta_k(t)$ is a finite signed state sum.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use one state of area $i$ and sign $(-1)^{i+k}$ for each $-k\leq i\leq k$. Alternatively, invoke signed universality and finite support.
\end{proof}

\section{Products and reciprocity}

Let $S,T$ be finite signed state families with signs $\sigma_S,\sigma_T$ and areas $a_S,a_T$. On $S\times T$, define
\[
 \sigma(s,u)=\sigma_S(s)\sigma_T(u),\qquad
 a(s,u)=a_S(s)+a_T(u).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Product--convolution theorem]
The signed generating function of the product state family is the product of the factor generating functions:
\[
 F_{S\times T}(t)=F_S(t)F_T(t).
\]
At degree $m$,
\[
 F_{S\times T}(m)=
 \sum_{s\in S}\sigma_S(s)F_T(m-a_S(s))
 =\sum_{r\in\Z}F_S(r)F_T(m-r).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the sum over $(s,u)$. A pair has total area $m$ exactly when $a_T(u)=m-a_S(s)$. Factoring $\sigma_S(s)$ gives the first formula; grouping by $r=a_S(s)$ gives Cauchy convolution.
\end{proof}

This has the same algebraic form as connected-sum multiplicativity,
\[
 \Delta_{K_1\#K_2}(t)=\Delta_{K_1}(t)\Delta_{K_2}(t).
\]

\begin{corollary}[Multiplicativity of total signed weight]
\[
 \sum_{(s,u)\in S\times T}\sigma_S(s)\sigma_T(u)
 =\left(\sum_{s\in S}\sigma_S(s)\right)
  \left(\sum_{u\in T}\sigma_T(u)\right).
\]
Equivalently, $F_{S\times T}(1)=F_S(1)F_T(1)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Distribute the finite double sum, or evaluate the product theorem at $t=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
An area-negating, sign-preserving involution on a signed state family is a map $\phi:S\to S$ such that
\[
 \phi(\phi(s))=s,\qquad a(\phi(s))=-a(s),\qquad
 \sigma(\phi(s))=\sigma(s).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Involution criterion for reciprocity]
If a finite signed state family admits an area-negating, sign-preserving involution, then its coefficient function is palindromic and
\[
 F(t)=F(t^{-1}).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The involution is a sign-preserving bijection from the area-$m$ fiber to the area-$(-m)$ fiber. Therefore their signed sums agree.
\end{proof}

If reflection sends area $a$ to $C-a$ instead, the same argument gives reciprocity up to a monomial shift. Recentring the grading yields the area-negating formulation when the grading allows it.

\section{Closed identities for $T(2,2k+1)$}

\begin{theorem}[Palindromicity]
For every $k\geq0$, $\Delta_k(t)=\Delta_k(t^{-1})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The support interval $[-k,k]$ is reflection invariant. The exponents $i+k$ and $-i+k$ differ by $2i$, so they have the same parity and hence the same coefficient.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization]
For every $k\geq0$, $\Delta_k(1)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There are $2k+1$ consecutive alternating coefficients, beginning and ending with $+1$. Pairing adjacent $1-1$ terms leaves one $+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minus-one evaluation and determinant]
For every $k\geq0$,
\[
 \Delta_k(-1)=(-1)^k(2k+1),
 \qquad |\Delta_k(-1)|=2k+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The degree-$i$ term contributes
\[
 (-1)^{i+k}(-1)^i=(-1)^{2i+k}=(-1)^k.
\]
There are $2k+1$ exponents in the support.
\end{proof}

These evaluations display two complementary effects of signs. At $t=1$ the coefficients almost completely cancel, leaving $1$. At $t=-1$ the evaluation factor aligns all signs, and every contribution reinforces.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{Path-area enumeration.}
Enumerate the $\binom{2n}{n}$ choices of north-step positions. Scan each word from left to right, maintaining the number of east steps already seen; upon reading a north step, add that number to the area. A dictionary indexed by area stores the counts. The direct method takes
\[
 O\!\left(n\binom{2n}{n}\right)
\]
time and $O(n^2)$ dictionary space. A forbidden predicate is evaluated before incrementing. Geometric local restrictions may admit dynamic programming, but an arbitrary predicate need not.

\paragraph{Universal signed construction.}
For each nonzero coefficient $c(m)$, output $|c(m)|$ states of area $m$ and sign $\sgn(c(m))$. If $N=\sum_m|c(m)|$, construction takes $O(N)$ time and output space. Under unit signs this output size is minimal.

\paragraph{Sparse convolution.}
For supports of sizes $r$ and $s$, iterate over occupied degree pairs and add $c(i)d(j)$ to degree $i+j$. The method takes $O(rs)$ time. It is the compressed coefficient-level counterpart of forming the Cartesian product of state families.

\paragraph{Reciprocity and evaluations.}
A hash-table representation permits a palindromicity test in expected linear time by comparing $c(i)$ with $c(-i)$. Generating the torus family and evaluating it at $\pm1$ both take $O(k)$ time.

\section{Examples}

\begin{example}[Unsigned paths of size two]
The six monotone paths from $(0,0)$ to $(2,2)$ have area distribution
\[
 1+t+2t^2+t^3+t^4.
\]
Every coefficient is nonnegative. Deleting paths can lower these entries but cannot make any one negative.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[First torus knots]
The first three nontrivial members are
\begin{align*}
 \Delta_1(t)&=t^{-1}-1+t,\\
 \Delta_2(t)&=t^{-2}-t^{-1}+1-t+t^2,\\
 \Delta_3(t)&=t^{-3}-t^{-2}+t^{-1}-1+t-t^2+t^3.
\end{align*}
Their values at $1$ are all $1$, while their values at $-1$ are $-3$, $5$, and $-7$, respectively.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Product states]
Squaring the trefoil polynomial gives
\[
 (t^{-1}-1+t)^2=t^{-2}-2t^{-1}+3-2t+t^2.
\]
The same coefficients arise from the nine ordered pairs of its three unit-sign states: areas add and signs multiply.
\end{example}

\section{Applications, limitations, and future work}

The positivity obstruction is a general model-selection principle. Whenever a proposed interpretation contributes one unweighted object per state, negative coefficients immediately refute it. In physical language, an unsigned generating function resembles a partition function with positive degeneracies, whereas a signed state sum resembles a graded trace or index in which sectors cancel. The Alexander polynomial belongs naturally to the latter class.

The universal construction is existential and coefficient-driven. It does not extract states from crossings, provide a canonical forbidden region, or prove Reidemeister invariance through local moves. Likewise, the product theorem explains what happens if product state spaces are used, but a diagram-level theory must still show that connected sum induces such a product. The involution criterion supplies a mechanism for reciprocity, not the required knot-specific involution.

The principal open problem is therefore a diagram-local signed path model in which each crossing determines local admissibility, area, and sign data, and Reidemeister moves induce explicit bijections or cancellations. For reduced alternating diagrams, the coefficientwise absolute value may admit an unsigned path interpretation after a grading shift, because the global alternating sign pattern can be separated from positive magnitudes. Reciprocity suggests a path reflection taking area $a$ to $C-a$, while connected sum suggests concatenation or Cartesian product of ensembles. Related signed constructions for Jones and HOMFLY polynomials may require larger step alphabets or multigradings.

\section{Conclusion}

Unsigned finite area generating functions are exactly the finitely supported Laurent polynomials with nonnegative integer coefficients; finite signed state sums are exactly all finitely supported integer Laurent polynomials. The family $T(2,2k+1)$ makes the difference unavoidable: each nontrivial member contains a coefficient $-1$, so no arbitrary selection of monotone paths can realize it as an unsigned count. Nevertheless, every member has a finite signed realization, is palindromic, evaluates to $1$ at $t=1$, and has determinant $2k+1$ from evaluation at $t=-1$.

Signed state spaces also carry the correct structural operations: Cartesian products produce Cauchy products, total signed weights multiply, and area-reversing sign-preserving involutions produce reciprocity. The corrected bridge between knots and lattices is therefore not ordinary counting, but counting with cancellation.

