# Knots, paths, and cancellation: an interactive journey

Begin with the motivating question: can a knot polynomial be an ordinary census of lattice paths? The [Alexander polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_polynomial) is a Laurent polynomial whose integer coefficients encode knot information, while [lattice-path generating functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_path) organize paths by statistics such as area.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The red coefficient cards reveal the obstruction. An unsigned count cannot produce a negative coefficient. This is not peculiar to one knot: every nontrivial member displayed has a coefficient $-1$.

## See positivity directly

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

The bars are genuine cardinalities, so deletion may lower them but never make them negative. The exhaustive procedure behind the chart is available here:

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the positivity argument</summary>
For an allowed path family $L$ with area $A$, the coefficient of $t^m$ in $\sum_{p\in L}t^{A(p)}$ is $\#\{p\in L:A(p)=m\}$. Every finite cardinality is a nonnegative integer. Allowing an arbitrary forbidden predicate only changes the subset $L$; it cannot change this conclusion.
</details>

## Repair the model with signs

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

Signed states contribute $+1$ or $-1$. For coefficient $c_m$, create $|c_m|$ states of area $m$, all having sign $\operatorname{sgn}(c_m)$. This represents every finitely supported integer Laurent polynomial and uses the fewest possible unit-sign states at each degree.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the universality proof</summary>
Only finitely many degrees are occupied. In each degree $m$, the constructed states sum to $\operatorname{sgn}(c_m)|c_m|=c_m$. Taking the finite union over all occupied degrees proves the claim.
</details>

## Products become pairs of states

Pair a state from the first model with one from the second. Add their areas and multiply their signs. The resulting coefficient rule is the [Cauchy product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauchy_product), the same algebraic operation governing multiplication of Laurent polynomials.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal reciprocity and the torus-family identities</summary>
A sign-preserving involution sending area $a$ to $-a$ bijects the degree-$m$ and degree-$(-m)$ fibers, so their signed sums agree. For $\Delta_k(t)=\sum_{i=-k}^k(-1)^{i+k}t^i$, reflection $i\mapsto-i$ preserves parity and proves reciprocity. The $2k+1$ alternating coefficients sum to $1$ at $t=1$; at $t=-1$ every term becomes $(-1)^k$, giving $\Delta_k(-1)=(-1)^k(2k+1)$.
</details>

## Continue exploring

Use {{demo:1}} to inspect unsigned area distributions and compare them with the alternating family. The corrected bridge is precise: unsigned finite state sums are exactly finitely supported nonnegative coefficient functions, while signed finite state sums are exactly all finitely supported integer coefficient functions. The next challenge is geometric rather than existential—extract the signs, paths, and symmetries canonically from knot diagrams.

